/* Editorial pass 18B: exact canonical chapter updates, loaded after pass 18A. */
(function(){'use strict';const library=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!library||!Array.isArray(library.series))return;const changes={"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-17":{"number":17,"title":"Benchwork","content":"Danse flirted like a man attempting to repair a radio with a combat knife.\n\nThat is to say: earnestly, dangerously, and without recognizing what he was doing.\n\nWe were at a weapon bench on the Prydwen when it started.\n\nI had Righteous Authority disassembled for another adjustment. Danse stood beside me sorting components.\n\n“You’ve improved,” he said.\n\n“At weapon modification?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You no longer install parts backward.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“There it is.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“You almost complimented me without insult.”\n\n“That wasn’t an insult. It was an observation.”\n\n“Your romance technique must be devastating.”\n\nThe component in his hand stopped moving.\n\nI realized what I had said.\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“No. You said something about…”\n\n“I was joking.”\n\nHis ears began to turn red.\n\n“Right.”\n\nI returned my attention to the rifle with the concentration of someone defusing a bomb.\n\nDanse did the same.\n\nFor approximately thirty seconds.\n\nThen he said, “For the record, I’m not attempting to romance you.”\n\nI nearly dropped the barrel.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Obviously.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nSilence.\n\nA scribe at the next bench quietly gathered her tools and left.\n\nCoward.\n\nDanse cleared his throat.\n\n“I only meant that your technical skills have improved.”\n\n“Understood.”\n\n“And I enjoy working with you.”\n\nMy hands stopped.\n\nHe seemed not to notice the second sentence was far more dangerous than the first.\n\n“I enjoy working with you too.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nAnother silence.\n\nHe picked up the wrong screw.\n\nI stared.\n\nDanse looked down, realized it, and set it back.\n\n“Don’t,” he said.\n\n“I did not say a word.”\n\n“You were going to.”\n\n“I notice patterns.”\n\nHe gave me a look that should have melted armor.\n\nInstead it made warmth spread through my chest.\n\nThat evening we took two beers to the observation deck.\n\nDanse had called it “decompression after duty.”\n\nI called it having a drink together.\n\nHe refused to call it anything.\n\nThe Commonwealth below was scattered with lights: Diamond City, settlements, campfires, places where people had decided darkness did not get everything.\n\nDanse leaned on the rail.\n\n“You ever think about leaving?” I asked.\n\n“The Brotherhood?”\n\n“The Commonwealth.”\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n“Where would I go?”\n\n“Anywhere.”\n\n“That’s not a destination.”\n\n“Neither is purpose.”\n\nHe took a drink.\n\n“You really dislike that word.”\n\n“I dislike when people use it instead of wanting something.”\n\n“Wanting isn’t always useful.”\n\n“Neither is beer.”\n\n“That’s debatable.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse’s smile stayed after mine faded.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You were looking at me.”\n\n“I do that sometimes.”\n\nMy own words, returned.\n\nThe air between us changed.\n\nNeither of us looked away.\n\nDanse’s face was different when he was not actively trying to be a paladin. Softer around the eyes. Younger. There was a small scar near one eyebrow I had never asked about. His mouth was more expressive than he seemed to realize.\n\nI became extremely aware of it.\n\nHe looked at mine too.\n\nThen a vertibird roared past the deck.\n\nThe moment broke.\n\nDanse looked over the rail.\n\n“I should check the duty roster.”\n\n“At twenty-two hundred?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHis head snapped toward me.\n\n“What did you say?”\n\n“You heard me.”\n\n“I am not a coward.”\n\n“Then stay.”\n\nI expected him to leave out of principle.\n\nInstead he sat on the bench beside me.\n\n“Fine.”\n\n“Outstanding.”\n\n“Don’t start.”\n\nWe stayed.\n\nThe conversation wandered into meaningless things, which somehow felt more intimate than confession.\n\nWorst pre-War food we had eaten. Danse claimed canned imitation crab was a crime against humanity. I argued that the Brotherhood should revise its mission statement accordingly.\n\nFavorite weapon modifications. Best places to sleep in the field. Whether a mirelurk counted as seafood.\n\n“It lives in the water,” I said.\n\n“So do things I would not eat.”\n\n“That is cowardice again.”\n\n“That word does not mean what you think it means.”\n\nAt some point our knees touched.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\nThe contact lasted long enough to become intentional.\n\nDanse looked down.\n\nThen at me.\n\n“I meant what I said earlier.”\n\n“About not romancing me?”\n\nHe shut his eyes briefly.\n\n“No. About enjoying working with you.”\n\n“Oh.”\n\nHis voice lowered.\n\n“It’s… comfortable.”\n\nFor Danse, that might have been a declaration.\n\nI swallowed.\n\n“I feel the same.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\nOur knees remained together.\n\nNo kiss.\n\nNo dramatic confession.\n\nJust the Prydwen humming around us and a soldier who feared attachment allowing one small point of contact to remain.\n\nEventually Danse stood.\n\n“Get some sleep.”\n\n“There’s the romance technique.”\n\nHe groaned.\n\nI grinned.\n\nAs he walked away he called back, “Good night.”\n\nNot civilian.\n\nNot soldier.\n\nJust good night.\n\nI sat there another minute smiling into my beer like an idiot.\n\nHis next attempt at flirting involved a torque wrench.\n\nI was replacing the recoil compensator on a combat rifle when Danse appeared at the bench and set a tool beside my elbow.\n\n“You’ll get better leverage with this.”\n\nI looked at the wrench.\n\nThen at him.\n\n“Did you come all the way across the Prydwen to critique my leverage?”\n\n“I was passing through.”\n\n“You are carrying two cups of coffee.”\n\nHe looked down as if surprised to find them.\n\n“One is for you.”\n\n“Is it drinkable?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That was not a confident answer.”\n\n“I added sugar.”\n\n“How much?”\n\nDanse became suspiciously interested in the rifle.\n\nI tasted it.\n\nThe coffee was still strong enough to strip paint, but someone had clearly attempted mercy.\n\n“Danse.”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“You remembered how I take it.”\n\nHis ears reddened.\n\n“I remember operational preferences.”\n\n“Sugar is tactical now?”\n\n“It affects morale.”\n\nI laughed so hard I had to set the cup down.\n\nDanse crossed his arms.\n\n“I fail to see what’s amusing.”\n\n“You have been flirting with me for weeks and somehow you’re doing it through logistics.”\n\nHis entire face stopped.\n\nI stopped laughing.\n\nOh.\n\nThere are moments when the Commonwealth seems to hold its breath just to see what stupid thing I will say next.\n\nDanse stared at me.\n\n“I have not—”\n\n“You don’t have to—”\n\n“I mean, I wasn’t—”\n\n“Right.”\n\n“Not intentionally.”\n\nThat shut us both up.\n\nHis eyes widened a fraction as he heard himself.\n\nI leaned against the bench.\n\n“Not intentionally?”\n\nDanse looked as though he would have preferred another super mutant battle.\n\n“I enjoy your company.”\n\nThe words emerged with the rigid precision of a formal report.\n\n“I had gathered that.”\n\n“I respect your judgment.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“Frequently.”\n\n“Generous.”\n\n“And I…”\n\nHe stopped.\n\nHis gaze dropped to the coffee cup.\n\n“I notice when you’re not around.”\n\nThe room suddenly felt too small.\n\nI let the teasing fall away.\n\n“I notice too.”\n\nDanse looked up.\n\nWe stood on opposite sides of a workbench covered in springs, screws, and a half-disassembled rifle.\n\nIt was not romantic by any reasonable standard.\n\nIt felt more intimate than a candlelit dinner could have.\n\nHe reached toward me, hesitated, then picked up the torque wrench instead.\n\n“This fastener is going to strip.”\n\nBefore he could escape into the fastener, I noticed a small cloth-wrapped bundle near his elbow.\n\n“What’s that?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Suspicious answer.”\n\n“It’s a component.”\n\n“For?”\n\nDanse hesitated, then unfolded the cloth. Inside was a polished beam focuser, cleaner than anything available in the common parts bins.\n\nI stared at it.\n\n“You requisitioned this.”\n\n“I located it.”\n\n“With paperwork?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For my rifle.”\n\n“For Righteous Authority.”\n\n“My rifle.”\n\nHe folded his arms. “You’ve been complaining about energy dispersion.”\n\n“I complained once.”\n\n“You were correct.”\n\nI picked up the focuser. “Is this a gift?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\n“It improves mission effectiveness.”\n\n“Is that what Brotherhood officers call flowers?”\n\nHis entire face turned red.\n\n“I have never given anyone flowers.”\n\n“I believe you.”\n\nHe reached for the part. I pulled it out of range.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nThe embarrassment eased enough for him to meet my eyes.\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\nThere it was again. Care translated into equipment because equipment had specifications and affection did not.\n\nI could work with that.\n\nI installed the new focuser while Danse watched over my shoulder.\n\n“Quarter turn,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re over-tightening it.”\n\n“I am not.”\n\nHe reached around me and put his hand over mine on the tool.\n\nThe correction became unnecessary almost immediately.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\nHis chest was warm against my back. His breath caught near my ear. For a man who could issue orders under gunfire without hesitation, Danse had no procedure for standing too close to someone he wanted.\n\n“Quarter turn?” I asked quietly.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You stopped turning.”\n\n“I noticed.”\n\nHis hand left mine with visible reluctance.\n\nThe rifle, at least, had the decency to remain uncomplicated.\n\nI stared.\n\nHe stared back.\n\n“That’s your retreat?”\n\n“I don’t know what you mean.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHis mouth opened.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\nActually laughed, head dropping for a second as the sound escaped him.\n\nIt was warm and rough and so rare I forgot the comeback I had prepared.\n\nWhen he recovered, he slid the wrench toward me.\n\n“Use the correct tool.”\n\nI took it.\n\nOur fingers brushed.\n\nNeither of us pretended not to notice.\n\nThe rifle took another twenty minutes.\n\nThe conversation took considerably longer.\n\n\nThree days after the incident with the wrench, Danse found me sorting ammunition at the workbench.\n\n\"I have a question,\" he said.\n\n\"Is it a test?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Are you certain? Because with you those categories overlap.\"\n\nHe set a small box of cleaned bolts beside me. He had labeled them by size and condition.\n\nThe case latch caught as I tried to slide it into place. Danse noticed before I swore at it.\n\n“Stop. The hinge is bent.”\n\n“I'm beginning to think the entire Commonwealth is bent.”\n\n“That is not an adequate inspection category.”\n\nHe took the case, checked the latch, then set it aside with a scrap of red cloth tied around its handle. He didn't try to force it shut. Every mistake at a bench had consequences, and I liked that he remembered that even when he was flustered.\n\n“Should I fetch another?” I asked.\n\n“Please.”\n\nI stopped moving. He noticed.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You said please.”\n\n“I am capable of courtesy.”\n\n“Right, but you usually issue it at parade-ground volume.”\n\nHis ears colored. “You are making this unnecessarily difficult.”\n\n“Only because you're worth annoying.”\n\nDanse opened his mouth, closed it, and checked the safe case twice before he spoke again. The thoroughness was familiar; the uncertainty was not.\n\n“I wasn't trained for conversations in which the answer cannot be evaluated against a written standard,” he admitted.\n\n“Most of us aren't.”\n\n“Then I would prefer to ask plainly, even if the answer is complicated.”\n\nHe set the cartridges aside and faced me with a gravity that might have been ridiculous if it weren't so sincere. I waited for the question. I knew whatever came next would sound like Danse, not like someone serenading me or handing me a parable about love.\n\n\n\"Are you currently involved with anyone?\"\n\nI nearly dropped a cartridge.\n\n\"That's direct.\"\n\n\"I thought you preferred directness.\"\n\n\"I do.\" I set the cartridge down. \"No, not at the moment. But there's something you should know if this is going where I think it might.\"\n\nThe tips of his ears turned red. \"And where is that?\"\n\n\"Somewhere with far more paperwork than either of us expected.\"\n\nHe folded his arms.\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I said. \"I prefer polyamory. For me that means I might have more than one loving relationship, with everyone fully informed and consenting. No hidden partners. No rules made behind somebody's back. And it means you would always get to decide what you were comfortable with.\"\n\nDanse stared at the box of bolts.\n\n\"I understand the definition,\" he said. \"I'm considering the implications.\"\n\n\"That is an extremely Danse answer.\"\n\n\"Would you expect me to share information about our relationship with other people?\"\n\n\"Not your private history. We would decide together what stays between us, what gets disclosed to someone directly affected, and what is nobody else's business. I won't share your trauma as a relationship update.\"\n\nHe gave that the thought it deserved.\n\n\"Would I be required to pursue other relationships?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Would you expect me not to experience jealousy?\"\n\n\"Also no. I'd expect you to talk to me rather than turning it into an order.\"\n\nHe looked almost offended. \"I don't issue orders about personal matters.\"\n\n\"You told me to eat lunch yesterday.\"\n\n\"That was a health recommendation.\"\n\n\"At parade-ground volume.\"\n\nA reluctant breath escaped him. It might have been a laugh.\n\n\"I was taught loyalty is singular,\" he said. \"One chain of command. One mission. One oath. I need to think about whether I've mistaken those rules for rules about people.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to think.\"\n\n\"And to say no?\"\n\n\"Yes. Even to me.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"Then I would like to keep seeing where this leads. I won't ask you to promise exclusivity. If my feelings change, I'll tell you rather than pretend.\"\n\nI felt the relief like someone had loosened a strap I'd forgotten was tight.\n\n\"One request,\" he said.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"No critical relationship discussions while handling live ammunition.\"\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nHe slid the ammunition case out of my reach. Then, very carefully, he placed his hand on the bench beside mine. An invitation, not a command. I let our little fingers touch.\n\nThe next time he told me to use the correct tool, I made a point of asking whether he had a form for romantic incompetence.\n\nHe said, \"Several.\"\n\nI believed him.","word_count":2229},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-36":{"number":36,"title":"Three Chords","content":"Danse learned three chords.\n\nIt took him two months.\n\nNot because the chords were difficult.\n\nBecause every time he made a mistake, he tried to turn music practice into military discipline.\n\n“Again,” he muttered.\n\nThe chord buzzed badly.\n\n“Relax your hand.”\n\n“I am relaxed.”\n\n“You’re gripping the neck like it insulted Maxson.”\n\nHis fingers loosened.\n\n“Again.”\n\n“See? You do listen to me.”\n\n“Occasionally.”\n\nWe sat on the porch of the rebuilt house. The borrowed guitar had become a permanent gift after the settlement musician found another instrument.\n\nDanse had resisted accepting it.\n\nThen spent six hours cleaning it.\n\nNow he played the first chord.\n\nThen the second.\n\nThen the third.\n\nAll clean.\n\nHe stopped.\n\nI grinned.\n\nDanse looked at his fingers.\n\n“Again.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“You did it.”\n\n“I can improve the transition speed.”\n\n“Tomorrow.”\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“I have momentum.”\n\n“You have a successful ending. Learn to recognize one.”\n\nThat landed deeper than I intended.\n\nDanse looked out over the road.\n\nA caravan was moving in the distance. Sunlight warmed the dead grass. Somewhere behind the house, our new windmill squeaked every third rotation.\n\n“I don’t know how,” he admitted.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Recognize an ending before something goes wrong.”\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n“You’re waiting for the next casualty.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The next order.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The next revelation that destroys your life.”\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\n“Dramatic.”\n\n“Accurate, though.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nI touched the guitar.\n\n“Three chords. Nothing went wrong.”\n\n“Yet.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe exhaled.\n\n“Three chords,” he said.\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Successful objective.”\n\n“Not an objective.”\n\n“Successful…”\n\nHe searched.\n\n“Thing.”\n\nI laughed.\n\n“Perfect.”\n\nHe set the guitar aside.\n\nThen pulled me into his lap with enough ease to remind me that civilian clothes had not changed how strong he was.\n\nI made a surprised noise.\n\nDanse looked pleased.\n\n“That was deliberate.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Just clarifying.”\n\nHis arms settled around me.\n\nWe watched the sunset.\n\nAfter a while I asked, “Do you miss the armor?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you miss being a paladin?”\n\nA longer pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“I don’t miss what I believed I had to be.”\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n“What was that?”\n\n“Certain.”\n\nThe answer surprised me.\n\nDanse looked down at his hands.\n\n“I thought doubt was weakness. Commanders can’t hesitate. Soldiers can’t question every threat. You make decisions and live with them.”\n\n“That part is still true.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But?”\n\n“But certainty became easier than thinking.”\n\nThat was the closest he had come to naming the thing that had almost killed him.\n\nI kissed his cheek.\n\n“Three chords.”\n\nHe smiled.\n\n“Three chords.”\n\nThe next day he learned a fourth.\n\nThe fourth chord created a problem.\n\nThree chords had been an experiment.\n\nFour meant Danse now believed he should be able to play a song.\n\nThis was how I woke one morning to the same six seconds of music repeated from the porch with increasing hostility.\n\nI pulled on a shirt and opened the door.\n\nDanse sat in the early light with the guitar balanced on one knee, red scarf looped loosely around his neck because the morning was cold.\n\nHe played the progression.\n\nStopped.\n\nPlayed it again.\n\nStopped.\n\n“Are you fighting the song?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You look like you’re fighting the song.”\n\n“The transition is too slow.”\n\n“It is six in the morning.”\n\n“That is unrelated.”\n\n“Nothing musical should happen at six in the morning unless somebody is being paid.”\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n“You’re awake now.”\n\n“Against my will.”\n\nI sat beside him anyway.\n\nHe played again.\n\nThis time I recognized the shape of an old pre-War tune we had heard on a trader’s radio.\n\n“You’ve been practicing in secret.”\n\n“I practice while you’re doing supply runs.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nHis fingers stopped.\n\nThe answer took long enough that I knew it mattered.\n\n“I wanted to surprise you.”\n\nThat was almost offensively sweet.\n\nI leaned my shoulder against his.\n\n“You did.”\n\n“It isn’t ready.”\n\n“I didn’t say it was good.”\n\nHe looked wounded.\n\nI laughed and kissed the corner of his jaw.\n\n“It’s getting there.”\n\nDanse stared at the strings.\n\n“I don’t know why I care whether it sounds good to you.”\n\n“Yes, you do.”\n\n“Fine. I don’t know why I care this much.”\n\n“That I believe.”\n\nHe set the guitar aside.\n\n“When I was in the Brotherhood, praise was measurable. Accuracy scores. completed objectives. promotions. A superior told you whether you met the standard.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“Now I play four chords on a porch and wait for your face to tell me whether it meant anything.”\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n“That sounds terrifying.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“We have discussed your inappropriate use of that word.”\n\n“I mean it’s vulnerable.”\n\n“That does not make it good.”\n\n“No. But you’re doing it anyway.”\n\nHe looked toward the road.\n\nThe windmill squeaked.\n\nA crow landed on the fence and immediately reconsidered the location when Danse picked the guitar up again.\n\n“This bird has better judgment than the dog,” I said.\n\n“Quiet.”\n\nHe began the progression.\n\nThis time he did not stop when one chord buzzed.\n\nHe kept going.\n\nThe song remained imperfect. The rhythm dragged. One transition arrived late enough that I could practically see him wanting to restart.\n\nHe did not.\n\nHe reached the end.\n\nThen looked at me.\n\nI clapped once.\n\n“Do not be patronizing.”\n\nI clapped louder.\n\nDanse tried not to smile.\n\nFailed.\n\n“You finished it,” I said.\n\n“I made three errors.”\n\n“And finished it.”\n\n“The timing—”\n\n“Finished.”\n\nHe breathed out.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThat afternoon the settlement held a small market. We brought repaired weapons, spare parts, and the guitar because one of the children had asked whether Danse would play.\n\nHe nearly refused.\n\nThen looked at me.\n\nI raised both hands.\n\n“Your choice.”\n\nHe hated that answer.\n\nHe played anyway.\n\nTen people listened. Nobody cared about the late chord. A little girl danced in circles with Captain the dog until both fell over.\n\nWhen Danse finished, the workshop owner cheered loudest.\n\nHe looked embarrassed.\n\nThen happy.\n\nNot proud because he had been best.\n\nProud because he had shared something unfinished and the world had not punished him for it.\n\nThe settlement asked him to play again the following week.\n\nDanse assumed they were being polite.\n\n“They heard the first performance,” he said. “They have sufficient information.”\n\n“Maybe they liked it.”\n\n“That would indicate poor judgment.”\n\nHe played anyway.\n\nThis time he made it through the first verse before missing the transition. His hand stopped automatically.\n\nNobody booed.\n\nA child near the fire shouted, “Keep going!”\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\nI lifted both hands. “Not an order.”\n\nHe looked back at the strings.\n\nThen continued from the next chord instead of restarting.\n\nThe mistake remained inside the song.\n\nSo did the song.\n\nWhen he finished, the applause was louder than before.\n\nDanse sat very still.\n\nLater an old farmer came over and said, “My wife used to love that tune.”\n\n“I played it badly,” Danse replied.\n\nThe farmer shrugged. “Still knew what it was.”\n\nDanse had no answer.\n\nOn the porch afterward, he told me that sentence bothered him.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I keep assuming imperfect means unrecognizable.”\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\n“And?”\n\n“And apparently that’s not always true.”\n\nHe played the troublesome transition once more.\n\nIt buzzed.\n\nHe did not start over.\n\nOne of the children from the settlement came over with a cheap instrument missing a tuning peg.\n\n“Can you fix this?” she asked Danse.\n\nHe looked at the damaged headstock as though she'd presented him with a malfunctioning weapon.\n\n“I can examine it,” he said. “I cannot guarantee a repair without the correct part.”\n\n“That's okay.”\n\nHe laid out a cloth to protect the finish and studied the fracture. His fingers were patient; his voice still had the roughness of a man accustomed to giving instructions in dangerous places.\n\n“You could ask Greg,” I said. “He knows instruments.”\n\n“Then I should ask him,” Danse replied. “Not invent expertise because someone expects me to be useful.”\n\nThere was a time when he would have treated admitting a limit as failure. Now he wrote down the part the child needed and asked her who actually owned the guitar. It was her mother's, she explained. They agreed to return it until the proper peg could be found.\n\n“You didn't fix it,” I told him when she left.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You helped.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe considered that, then laughed once, almost surprised by the sound.\n\n“Apparently there are successful outcomes that do not involve completing the task myself.”\n\nI raised an eyebrow. “Careful. That's dangerously close to a philosophy.”\n\n“Then strike it from the record,” he said.\n\nHe reached for the guitar case. The child had not given him a medal or a new order. She'd trusted him with a small problem, and he'd been careful enough not to make it larger.\n\n\nAfter the applause, Danse packed the guitar as carefully as he once packed a laser rifle.\n\nThe old farmer stopped him again.\n\n“Come by next week,” the man said. “I know another song you might like.”\n\nDanse’s first instinct was visible: refuse before obligation could form.\n\nThen he glanced at me.\n\nNot for permission.\n\nFor orientation.\n\n“I might,” he said.\n\nThe farmer nodded as if *might* were enough.\n\nOn the road, I bumped my shoulder against his.\n\n“Look at you making social plans.”\n\n“I made no commitment.”\n\n“You said might.”\n\n“That is explicitly noncommittal.”\n\n“For you, that’s practically spontaneous.”\n\nHe muttered something about inaccurate standards.\n\nBut his hand found mine anyway.\n\nHalfway down the road he played the troublesome transition while walking.\n\nIt was worse than before.\n\nHe laughed first.\n\nDanse looked back once toward the fire, where someone else had started another song. He did not look embarrassed anymore. Mostly, he looked as though he wanted to remember the sound.\n\nThe strings had left shallow marks across his fingertips. He studied them with absurd satisfaction, like evidence that the afternoon had actually happened.\n\n“Worth it?” I asked.\n\n“Yes,” he said, and did not qualify the answer.\n\nOn the walk home, he carried the guitar over one shoulder.\n\n“You know what the worst part is?” he asked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“I want to learn the whole song.”\n\nI gasped theatrically.\n\n“Ambition.”\n\n“And another after that.”\n\n“Dangerous.”\n\n“I may even learn one you like.”\n\nI took his free hand.\n\n“I already like this one.”\n\n“It was badly played.”\n\n“That wasn’t what I said.”\n\nDanse stopped.\n\nHe recognized the sentence.\n\nOne of his, from the beginning.\n\nThe smile came slowly.\n\n“Fair point.”\n\nWe kept walking.\n\nBehind us the settlement noise faded.\n\nAhead, our house waited with a porch large enough for two people, one guitar, and as many imperfect songs as he wanted to learn.\n","word_count":1772}},"after-always":{"after-always-09":{"number":9,"title":"A Cup of Tea, Unpoisoned","content":"The Floo network failed while we were halfway back to Hogwarts.\n\nThat sentence makes the experience sound almost reasonable.\n\nIt was not.\n\nOne moment I was spinning through green fire with a case of Snape's journals clutched to my chest. The next, the fireplace threw me out into a pub I had never seen, directly into a basket of umbrellas.\n\nSnape arrived three seconds later with considerably more dignity and considerably less balance.\n\nI caught his elbow.\n\nHe caught the mantel.\n\nA woman behind the bar looked up from polishing a glass.\n\n“Network's down north of Birmingham,” she said. “Storm charms. Could be an hour.”\n\nSnape closed his eyes.\n\nI pulled an umbrella out from under my knee.\n\n“Well,” I said. “At least we aren't dead.”\n\nHe opened one eye.\n\n“You have set an alarmingly low standard for travel.”\n\n“It has served me well.”\n\nThe pub was called The Copper Kettle. It occupied the back half of what looked from the street like a closed tailor's shop. There were six tables, a crooked bar, and a fire that smelled of cedar.\n\nWe took the table furthest from everyone else.\n\nNaturally.\n\nThe woman brought tea without asking.\n\nSnape looked at the cup.\n\nThen at her.\n\n“I remember you,” she said.\n\nHis face went blank.\n\n“You came through here in ninety-four. Bought a vial of salamander blood from my brother and told him his preservation charm was embarrassing.”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“He fixed it.”\n\n“Then the humiliation was educational.”\n\nShe laughed and walked away.\n\nI stared at him.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You leave a trail of improved storage charms and emotional damage everywhere you go.”\n\n“A legacy requires consistency.”\n\nThe tea was ordinary black tea, strong enough to be useful.\n\nI added milk.\n\nSnape did not.\n\nI pointed at his cup. “Are you going to test it?”\n\nHis eyebrow lifted.\n\n“For poison,” I said.\n\n“I understood the question.”\n\n“Given your life, it seems reasonable.”\n\nHe picked up the cup and drank.\n\n“No?”\n\n“If someone wished to poison me in a provincial pub, they would deserve the success.”\n\n“That is not a healthy attitude.”\n\n“It is an efficient one.”\n\nI sighed.\n\nHe looked faintly satisfied.\n\nOutside, rain streaked the windows. The delay gave us nowhere to go and nothing useful to do. I could have read one of the journals, but that felt intrusive while he sat across from me.\n\nSo I watched the storm.\n\nSnape watched me.\n\n“You dislike idle time,” he said.\n\n“So do you.”\n\n“I asked first.”\n\n“No, you made an observation.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“Yes,” I said. “I dislike it.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nThat surprised me.\n\nHe almost never asked questions that were not tactical.\n\nI considered giving him something easy.\n\nInstead I said, “If I'm doing something, I know what I'm for.”\n\nThe words sounded too honest once they existed.\n\nSnape looked at his tea.\n\n“Utility is not identity.”\n\nI laughed once.\n\n“That's rich coming from you.”\n\nHis eyes lifted.\n\n“Explain.”\n\n“You spent years making yourself useful to people who could not know what you were doing. Dumbledore needed a spy. Voldemort needed a servant. Hogwarts needed a teacher. Slytherin needed a Head of House. Harry needed someone keeping him alive from the shadows.”\n\nI stopped.\n\nSnape's face had gone very still.\n\n“I've read the public testimony,” I added. “Not your memories.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“And now you're recovering by turning yourself into an evidence archive with legs.”\n\nHis lip curled.\n\n“Poetic.”\n\n“Accurate.”\n\n“Presumptuous.”\n\n“Also accurate.”\n\nThe woman behind the bar dropped a spoon. The clatter filled the pause.\n\nAt last Snape said, “And what are you when you are not useful?”\n\nThe question found a place I had not armored.\n\nI looked out the window.\n\n“Annoying, apparently.”\n\n“That is a skill, not an identity.”\n\nI smiled despite myself.\n\n“I'm still figuring it out.”\n\nHe nodded once.\n\nNo advice.\n\nNo lecture.\n\nNo attempt to fix me.\n\nI appreciated it more than reassurance.\n\nThe storm worsened.\n\nSomeone near the bar started a card game. A wireless crackled with a news report about reconstruction grants and Ministry hearings. Snape's shoulders tightened when his name was mentioned.\n\nThe announcer called him “the controversial former Headmaster.”\n\nI reached over and turned the wireless down.\n\nSnape looked at my hand.\n\n“I am capable of hearing criticism.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Then why?”\n\n“Because I was trying to drink tea, and the man's voice is irritating.”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Accurate,” Snape said.\n\nI smiled.\n\nThe woman brought sandwiches.\n\n“We didn't order these,” I said.\n\n“House rule during network outages.”\n\nSnape eyed the plate.\n\n“Unpoisoned?” I asked.\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\nI took the first bite.\n\n“Heroic,” he said.\n\n“Someone has to protect you.”\n\nHis expression changed so quickly I almost missed it.\n\nThe joke had hit something real.\n\nI put the sandwich down.\n\n“I didn't mean—”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHis voice was quiet.\n\nThat should have ended it.\n\nInstead he said, “People have protected me.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“Few did so without expecting a return.”\n\n“Dumbledore?”\n\nHe looked at me sharply.\n\n“You said few. Not none.”\n\nHis gaze dropped.\n\n“Albus expected a great deal.”\n\n“That doesn't answer the question.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI waited again.\n\nHe seemed annoyed by my refusal to fill the silence.\n\nAt last he said, “He kept me alive when I gave him little reason to.”\n\n“And then used you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Both can be true.”\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nIt was one of the quietest agreements we had ever reached.\n\nI picked up my sandwich.\n\nAfter a moment, he picked up his.\n\nWe ate.\n\nThe network remained down for another ninety minutes.\n\nDuring that time, I learned that Snape hated sweet tea, preferred coffee but considered most public coffee an offense, could play chess but found competitive chess players tedious, and had once brewed a hangover cure for Professor McGonagall after a staff Christmas party.\n\nHe denied the last detail as soon as he realized he had told me enough to make it funny.\n\n“You are not repeating that.”\n\n“To anyone?”\n\n“To anyone.”\n\n“Can I repeat it to you?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“What if I write it down?”\n\nHis stare promised consequences.\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe looked at me over the rim of his cup.\n\nThere was something strange in his face.\n\nNot warmth exactly.\n\nAttention.\n\nThe kind that made me suddenly aware of the shape of my hands around the teacup and the rain cooling the window beside us.\n\nMy pulse changed.\n\nSnape's eyes narrowed.\n\nOf course he noticed.\n\nHe noticed everything.\n\nI looked away first.\n\nThat was new.\n\nThe bartender came back to tell us that the network inspectors had still not cleared the northbound hearth.\n\n“People keep asking whether it's safe,” she said. “If I knew that, I'd have gone home an hour ago.”\n\nSnape set his cup down.\n\n“Then stop telling them an hour,” he said. “Tell them it is closed until inspected.”\n\nShe gave him a narrow look. “Are you volunteering to explain that to my customers?”\n\n“No. You are far more qualified to tell them they're not getting what they want.”\n\nIt sounded insulting until he passed her the official delay notice he'd copied from the Ministry board outside. There was no comforting lie in it, only the relevant route and the name of the office responsible for approving travel.\n\nShe read it and nodded. “That will help.”\n\n“Good. Because I refuse to be scattered across three counties to spare an impatient stranger five minutes.”\n\nI laughed. Severus looked at me with weary suspicion.\n\n“You find that amusing?”\n\n“Only the part where you describe surviving a transport incident as an administrative inconvenience.”\n\n“It is an administrative inconvenience,” he said. “Potentially a terminal one.”\n\nHe examined his tea with obvious disapproval but did not abandon the cup. Watching him bristle over a faulty system reminded me that he was not a soft-spoken sage in black robes. He was sharp, exacting, petty when tired, and unexpectedly generous with information that could keep somebody else safe. I found the combination more compelling than I should have.\n\n\nThe Floo flared green.\n\n“Northbound connections restored,” the bartender called.\n\nWe stood.\n\nSnape lifted the lighter case.\n\nI took the heavier one.\n\nHe did not argue.\n\nAt the hearth, he paused.\n\n“The tea was poor,” he said.\n\n“It was fine.”\n\n“It was tannic.”\n\n“So are you.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nThen the corner of his mouth lifted.\n\n“After you.”\n\nI stepped into the green fire.\n\nAs the world began to spin, I realized I had just spent two hours trapped in a pub with Severus Snape and had not once wished for the network to return sooner.\n\nThat was probably a problem.\n\nThe delay gave us time to read three of the journals properly. Two were worthless inventories. The third contained a set of notes from a St Mungo's brewer who had studied long-term nerve damage after venom exposure.\n\nSnape read the same paragraph four times.\n\n“You've found something,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You've been glaring at that page like it owes you money.”\n\nHe slid the journal across the table.\n\nThe treatment was experimental, old, and probably useless. It was also the first reference either of us had found to a tremor pattern that resembled his.\n\n“Worth asking Pomfrey?”\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“That's practically enthusiasm.”\n\nHis finger tapped the margin. “It is a twenty-seven-year-old case series with six patients.”\n\n“So: not magic.”\n\n“Despite the setting, evidence remains advisable.”\n\nI smiled. “I'll copy the citation.”\n\nHe looked at me oddly.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You did not say it would work.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Most people do.”\n\nI understood then. Hope had become another thing people tried to force on him, as if optimism were a debt he owed for surviving.\n\n“It might help,” I said. “That's enough to investigate.”\n\nHis gaze lowered to the journal.\n\n“Yes,” he said. “It is.”\n\nI carried it back to Hogwarts with the journals.","word_count":1627},"after-always-44":{"number":44,"title":"Three Unsigned Letters","content":"The three letters arrived in an envelope that had been opened before it reached us.\n\nThere was no attempt to hide it. The seal had been cut cleanly, and the flap was folded back with the tidy care of somebody who believed procedure excused curiosity.\n\nSeverus held it beneath the archive lamp.\n\n\"I see,\" he said.\n\nI had learned that those two words could mean anything from mild irritation to the beginning of a personal storm.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"The committee.\"\n\nHe showed me the attached receipt. The Lanternweald Historical Committee had accepted a packet of wartime correspondence for review, then forwarded three items it considered relevant to Professor Severus Snape. The note asked him to identify the writers, confirm which were authentic, and authorize public display before the upcoming Closed Bells exhibit.\n\n\"Did they ask you whether they could open the packet?\" I said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Did the sender?\"\n\n\"The sender requested the letters be delivered to me. Not to the committee.\"\n\nI looked at the paper again.\n\nThe request was written in small print beneath a list of accession numbers, as though permission were a box that could be ticked by someone who had never been afraid of an envelope.\n\n\"What do you want to do?\" I asked.\n\nHe placed the letters on the table without opening them.\n\n\"At present? Break something.\"\n\n\"Anything in particular?\"\n\n\"The assumption that an archive is entitled to the contents of every grief it can find.\"\n\nI wanted to make a joke about how expensive that assumption would be to break, but the expression on his face stopped me.\n\nNeri came to the workroom when I sent for them. They listened while Severus explained what had happened. Their cheeks reddened.\n\n\"The committee meant to preserve the chain of custody,\" they said.\n\n\"Then perhaps,\" Severus replied, \"they should have preserved the seals.\"\n\nNeri flinched.\n\nI watched them closely. The Closed Bells history had already shown how people could turn safety into control when they grew frightened. Here was a quieter version: a group deciding that historical importance outranked the person whose name was on a letter.\n\n\"What is the actual rule?\" I asked.\n\nNeri sighed.\n\n\"There is no written rule about personal mail in the archives.\"\n\n\"Then this is where we write one.\"\n\nSeverus glanced at me. He did not look grateful yet.\n\nHe looked tired.\n\nWe brought the committee chair to the archive after lunch. They were a brisk person named Vale, with ink-stained fingers and a habit of consulting a notebook while others spoke.\n\n\"The letters concern a war,\" Vale said. \"They could be historically significant.\"\n\n\"So could my grocery lists,\" Severus replied. \"Would you like to display those too?\"\n\nVale looked at me for help.\n\nI did not give it.\n\n\"Whether something matters to history isn't the same question as whether you have permission to open it,\" I said. \"The sender's instructions were clear.\"\n\n\"We were afraid the packet might contain an active threat.\"\n\n\"Then you should have used an agreed safety process and documented it,\" Neri said. \"Not turned a private letter into reading material for the entire committee.\"\n\nVale's face tightened.\n\n\"That wasn't our intention.\"\n\n\"Then we need a process that doesn't depend on intention,\" I answered.\n\nSeverus was silent for a long time. I could see him weighing whether the discussion was worth participating in at all.\n\nAt last he said, \"Return the original seals, document who opened the packet, and withdraw the request for public display. I will decide whether to read the letters. That is not a decision I will outsource.\"\n\nVale looked as if they wanted to object.\n\nNeri spoke first.\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nIt took another hour to write the incident report. No one enjoyed it. That was probably a sign that we were finally doing the work.\n\nAfter the committee left, Severus took the letters to the small room above the archive. It was a place with one window, two chairs, and a table too narrow for comfort. He had once called it an architectural error. Today he asked me to sit there with him.\n\n\"Do you want me to stay while you read them?\"\n\n\"I want you here. I may not want to speak.\"\n\n\"That works.\"\n\nHe opened the first letter.\n\nHis expression did not change much. His thumb pressed harder against the paper.\n\nHe read the second, then placed it face down.\n\nFor a while he left the third unopened.\n\nI watched the light crawl across the window frame. From the square below came the faint noise of market carts and a distant argument about cabbages.\n\n\"Do you want me to make tea?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nI waited.\n\nEventually Severus spoke.\n\n\"The first is from someone who mistook obedience for loyalty. The second is from someone who knew better and was too frightened to act.\"\n\nI did not ask which people.\n\n\"And the third?\" I said.\n\nHe touched the sealed edge.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"You're allowed not to open it today.\"\n\nHis eyes moved to mine.\n\n\"That is an irritatingly simple statement.\"\n\n\"One of my gifts.\"\n\nHe looked down at the paper again.\n\n\"For years I believed that every mistake had to be accounted for at once. If I delayed, I was shirking a duty. If I spoke, someone might use the words against me. There was no correct move.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I am unsure whether that was duty or the shape fear took when I called it discipline.\"\n\nI didn't have a clever answer. Sometimes the most loving thing I could do was not invent one.\n\nI took his hand where it rested beside the unopened letter.\n\n\"You can choose tomorrow,\" I said. \"Or never. If there's a genuine danger to other people, we handle that. But you don't owe anyone your private thoughts as a public exhibit.\"\n\nHe breathed in, slowly.\n\n\"Will you ask me about the contents later?\"\n\n\"Only if you invite me.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"How do you manage to be both relentlessly curious and intolerably restrained?\"\n\n\"I contain multitudes.\"\n\n\"An unfortunate number.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe did not quite smile back, but his hand tightened around mine.\n\nNeri returned with a replacement accession receipt. This one had an empty box where the committee had previously assumed permission.\n\nSeverus stared at it.\n\n“An improvement,” he said.\n\n“Is that praise?” I asked.\n\n“Do not become accustomed to it.”\n\nNeri explained that the committee had separated preservation from publication. The packet could be protected from moisture without becoming an exhibit. The names of the senders would stay off the public index until the recipients decided what could be disclosed.\n\n“And who signs the access request?” Severus asked.\n\n“You do, if you choose to. The committee chair cannot sign on your behalf.”\n\nHe took the pen, turned the paper over, and wrote *No public access granted* beneath the receipt. His handwriting looked as though it had been designed to discourage argument.\n\n“Subtle,” I said.\n\n“I find ambiguity has been extravagantly overfunded.”\n\nNeri almost smiled. They accepted the form without asking him to explain the letters.\n\nWhen we were alone, I reached for his hand. He let me touch his fingers but kept the sealed envelope close. I had seen him fight over less consequential things than privacy; this time the victory was not making someone else confess. It was letting a private story remain private even when the archive could have displayed it beautifully.\n\n“Thank you,” he said.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“For not treating curiosity as a right.”\n\nI kissed his knuckles. His eyes narrowed, but he did not pull away.\n\n\nWe left the third letter sealed. He wrote a short note to the sender thanking them for trusting him with the packet, and he asked Neri to return the committee's original receipt with a correction. The letters would remain private unless Severus chose otherwise.\n\nThat evening we walked to Spinner's End through the Moonroot Door. The house smelled of rain and old wood. He took off his coat, hung it neatly, and stopped in the hallway.\n\n\"I was afraid,\" he said.\n\n\"Today?\"\n\n\"That you would think I was hiding something from you.\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"You are allowed to have things that belong to you.\"\n\n\"Even if you love me?\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand again. His fingers were cool from the walk. When he lifted them to his mouth, the kiss was brief and oddly reverent.\n\n\"Stay?\" he asked.\n\n\"I'd like to.\"\n\nHe nodded, and something in his expression eased.\n\nWe made tea. We did not talk about the letters over dinner. Later, when he kissed me beside the kitchen door, the familiar sharpness in his manner had gone quiet enough for me to notice how carefully he was listening for my answer.\n\n\"Yes,\" I whispered before he asked.\n\nHe smiled against my cheek.\n\nThe lamps burned low. The rest of the evening remained ours.\n\nThe next morning, the sealed letter lay exactly where he had left it.\n\nSome things were not meant to be opened merely because someone had found the key.","word_count":1505}},"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-08":{"number":8,"title":"Jam Session","content":"The invitation to the jam session arrived on a paper napkin.\n\nGreg left it beneath my apartment door with a doodle of a guitar, a star, and what might have been a pancake wearing sunglasses. The message read: FRIDAY. COMMUNITY CENTER. BRING YOURSELF. OPTIONAL SNACKS. PLEASE DO NOT BRING A JUDGMENTAL SEAGULL.\n\nI brought myself and a bag of cinnamon rolls.\n\nThe community center was a low building near the beach with bright murals on the exterior. Inside, folding chairs had been pushed against the walls. Instruments filled the room: guitars, hand drums, a keyboard missing one key, and a collection of shakers that looked as though they had been assembled from kitchen supplies.\n\nGreg stood in the middle of it all, arguing with a woman in a yellow jacket about the correct way to arrange the chairs.\n\n“The circle is symbolic,” he said.\n\n“The circle is blocking the outlet,” she replied.\n\n“The outlet is part of the circle.”\n\nShe saw me and pointed. “Tell him.”\n\nI looked at Greg. “The outlet is part of the circle.”\n\nHe beamed. “See?”\n\n“That was not what I said.”\n\n“Your support means everything.”\n\nThe woman shook her head and moved the chairs. Greg crossed the room to me, smiling in a way that made it difficult to remember I had planned to act normal.\n\n“You came.”\n\n“You left a napkin under my door.”\n\n“A very persuasive napkin.”\n\nI held out the cinnamon rolls. “Peace offering for the outlet.”\n\n“I knew I loved you.”\n\n“You loved me before the pastries.”\n\n“Now I love you with evidence.”\n\nThe other musicians greeted me. Some knew Greg well. Some knew him only as the man who arrived early, brought snacks, and occasionally tried to turn equipment failures into songs. I was given a place in the circle and a shaker made from a plastic container filled with beans.\n\n“I don’t play,” I whispered to Greg.\n\n“Neither do half the people here.”\n\n“That is not reassuring.”\n\n“Music is not a test. It’s a conversation.”\n\n“I am bad at conversations.”\n\n“You’re excellent at them. You just think too hard before speaking.”\n\nThe session began with a rhythm simple enough to follow. Greg played guitar. The woman in the yellow jacket played hand drum. Someone else hummed a melody that wandered through the room and came back wearing different colors.\n\nI shook the container on the beat.\n\nAt first, I counted. One, two, three, four. My shoulders stayed tight. My eyes kept checking the other people, measuring whether I was too loud, too quiet, too slow.\n\nThen Greg looked at me.\n\nHe did not nod in correction. He smiled.\n\nThe rhythm was not a set of rules I could fail. It was something happening around me, leaving room for me to join.\n\nI stopped counting.\n\nThe song changed.\n\nAfterward, I realized I had been singing under my breath. Greg had heard me. Of course he had.\n\n“You have a beautiful voice,” he said.\n\n“You’re biased.”\n\n“I’m also a professional.”\n\n“You’re a professional musician, not a professional judge of my throat.”\n\n“I can be both if I take a certification course.”\n\n“Please don’t.”\n\nHe laughed and touched my wrist. “Would you sing with me?”\n\nThe room was still noisy around me, but the question made it narrow.\n\n“In front of everyone?”\n\n“Only if you want.”\n\n“What would I sing?”\n\n“Whatever you like.”\n\n“What if I choose something terrible?”\n\n“Then I’ll make it sound intentional.”\n\nI looked at the guitars, the chairs, the people who were busy talking and tuning. Nobody was watching me except Greg.\n\n“Maybe one song,” I said.\n\n“I’m learning that word has range.”\n\nHe led me to the small keyboard. One of the keys was missing, but the rest worked. Greg stood beside me, close enough that my arms brushed when I shifted.\n\n“You can choose the key,” he said.\n\n“I don’t know keys.”\n\n“You know the song.”\n\nHe played a few chords. I recognized the shape. It was the song I had told him about at the diner, the one that had found me on a bad day.\n\n“You remembered.”\n\n“I remember a lot about you.”\n\nThe room began to quiet as the others noticed. Panic flashed through me.\n\nGreg saw it. “We can stop.”\n\nI looked at the keyboard. Then at him.\n\n“No,” I said. “Ask me again.”\n\nHis eyes softened. “Do you want to sing this with me?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe began the first line. His voice was warm and steady. I joined on the next phrase, quietly at first. The song carried my voice into the room and returned it changed, larger without becoming less mine.\n\nI did not look at the audience. I looked at Greg.\n\nHe watched me as though my voice were a secret he had been trusted to hold. When I stumbled over a note, he moved with me rather than correcting me. When I found the melody again, his smile widened.\n\nBy the final verse, I was singing.\n\nThe room applauded. Someone whistled. The woman in the yellow jacket called it beautiful and told Greg not to ruin it with a bridge.\n\nHe placed one hand over his heart. “I would never.”\n\n“You absolutely would,” I said.\n\n“Not in front of my beloved.”\n\nMy cheeks warmed.\n\nAfter the session, people shared food around a long table. Greg sat beside me, his knee touching mine beneath it. He did not move away. Every so often, his thumb brushed my knuckles where my hands rested together.\n\n“You were good,” he said.\n\n“You already told me.”\n\n“I have more evidence now.”\n\n“You are impossible.”\n\n“You love me.”\n\n“Unfortunately.”\n\n“Fortunately.”\n\nThe word was so certain that I looked at him.\n\nGreg's expression changed. “Sorry. That sounded like I was telling you how to feel.”\n\n“No.” I turned my hand beneath his, threading my fingers together. “It sounded like you were grateful.”\n\n“I am.”\n\nHe lifted my joined hands and kissed the back of mine. The gesture was visible to everyone, but there was no performance in it. He was not showing me off. He was simply loving me in the open.\n\nMy chest tightened with a happiness that frightened me.\n\n“What?” he asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“That’s your face.”\n\n“What face?”\n\n“The one where something is beautiful and you’re waiting for it to be taken away.”\n\nI looked down at the table.\n\nGreg waited.\n\n“I don’t know how to have something good without preparing for the end,” I said.\n\n“You can prepare for the end and still enjoy the middle.”\n\n“That sounds exhausting.”\n\n“It is.” He squeezed my hand. “But we can take turns.”\n\nI looked at him again.\n\n“What does that mean?”\n\n“When you’re waiting for the end, I’ll remind you that we’re in the middle. When I’m scared that I’ll disappoint you, you can remind me that I’m still here.”\n\n“You get scared?”\n\n“Constantly. I’m just tall enough to hide it.”\n\nI laughed. He leaned close and kissed my temple.\n\n“There’s one more thing,” he said.\n\n“Is it about music?”\n\n“Kind of.”\n\nHe reached into his pocket and pulled out a small metal pick. It was worn smooth, engraved with a tiny star.\n\n“My son gave me this years ago,” Greg said. “I keep losing it and finding it again. It’s not valuable, but it helps me remember that songs don’t belong to the person who starts them. They keep changing hands.”\n\nHe placed it in my palm.\n\n“I can’t take that,” I said.\n\n“You’re not taking it. I’m giving it to you.”\n\n“What if I lose it?”\n\n“Then we find it again.”\n\nMy eyes stung.\n\nGreg leaned in. “Do you want to go somewhere quiet?”\n\nI nodded.\n\nOutside, the night smelled like salt and warm pavement. The community center lights glowed behind me. Greg slipped an arm around my shoulders. I leaned into him as I walked toward the water.\n\nAt the beach, he sat in the sand and pulled me between his knees. The metal pick rested safely in my pocket.\n\n“You sang,” he murmured.\n\n“I did.”\n\n“You looked terrified.”\n\n“I was.”\n\n“You did it anyway.”\n\nI turned my head. “You’re going to make this into a lesson.”\n\n“I’m going to make it into a kiss.”\n\n“That sounds more useful.”\n\nGreg kissed me beneath the stars. His hands moved with familiar care. When I shifted onto his lap, he asked. When I said yes, he held me closer.\n\nThe tide whispered against the shore. We kept kissing until the cold drove us back toward the road. We lingered on the sand while Greg searched for his missing shoe. He found it under the guitar case and shook out a small beach's worth of sand.\n\n“Great,” he said. “Former rock star, defeated by footwear.”\n\nI laughed, then stopped. “Before this becomes a song with a surprise ending, I need to tell you something.”\n\nHe hooked the guitar strap over one shoulder. “Okay. I'm listening.”\n\n“I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I can love more than one person, and I won't ask someone to date me under the impression that I'll eventually become exclusive.”\n\nGreg tucked his hair behind an ear. It promptly fell out again.\n\n“Wow. That's clear.” He hesitated. “I want to be clear too. I've done the thing where I act cool with everything because I'm afraid somebody will leave. Not my greatest hit.”\n\n“You don't have to agree tonight.”\n\n“Good. 'Cause I like you, but I've got questions. And some of 'em might be dumb.”\n\n“Ask them anyway.”\n\n“Do we have to make a calendar?”\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“Oh, man. My natural enemy.”\n\nHe grinned, then took a breath. “Seriously, though. I'd rather figure out what I mean than say yes because you're cute and regret not being honest later.”\n\nI held out my hand. He took it. There was no instant agreement, no miraculous cure for jealousy, just a musician who could admit he was scared without pretending to be a philosopher.\n\nWe walked back to town with several questions still unanswered. I liked that he trusted me enough to leave them open.\n\n\nGreg walked me to the motel, kissed me goodnight at the door, and made me promise to text when I got inside.\n\nThe next morning, Greg sent me a voice message before he sent a text. His voice was sleep-rough and too close to the microphone.\n\n“Good morning,” he said. “I’m leaving this because I wanted you to hear me say that you sounded beautiful last night. Also because I burned toast and need someone to blame.”\n\nI listened twice before answering.\n\nYOU BURNT THE TOAST YOURSELF.\n\nHis reply arrived almost immediately.\n\nI WAS EMOTIONALLY DISTRACTED BY YOUR TALENT.\n\nI smiled at my phone. The warmth lasted through breakfast, through the walk to the community center, and into the afternoon when I found the plastic shaker in my bag.\n\nI had taken it without thinking. The container was cheap. The beans inside made a dry, uneven sound when I moved it. Still, it felt like proof that I had joined something.\n\nAt the next session, I placed it on the table instead of hiding it in my pocket.\n\nThe woman in the yellow jacket noticed. “You keeping that?”\n\n“Apparently.”\n\n“Good. It has personality.”\n\nGreg arrived late with a box of pastries and a guilty expression. He looked at the shaker, then at me.\n\n“You brought your instrument.”\n\n“It has a personality problem.”\n\n“That makes two of us.”\n\nHe kissed my forehead in front of the circle. The gesture was quick, but it made me feel seen without making me feel displayed.\n\nWhen the music started, I found the beat faster this time. I still missed it occasionally. Greg still smiled. The song kept moving.\n\nAfterward, he tucked the shaker into my bag with exaggerated care.\n\n“You know,” he said, “we can find you a better one.”\n\n“This one has history.”\n\n“It’s been used once.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nHe looked at me with that warm, attentive expression. “I like seeing you collect evidence that you belong here.”\n\nI touched the metal pick in my pocket. “I like that you notice.”\n\n“I told you.”\n\n“You remember a lot.”\n\n“I do.”\n\nI kissed him under the community center awning while the last of the musicians carried instruments past me. The kiss was not a performance. It was simply another note in the conversation.","word_count":2050},"su-35":{"number":35,"title":"The Note in the Glovebox","content":"The glovebox would not close.\n\nGreg had spent ten minutes trying to make the map fit around an old flashlight, three receipts, a packet of guitar picks, and a folded piece of paper I had never seen before.\n\n\"What's that?\" I asked.\n\nHe froze for half a second.\n\n\"Old stuff.\"\n\n\"Do you want help sorting it?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nI didn't reach for the paper.\n\nWe were parked near Beach City, waiting for the rain to ease before driving up to the overlook. The inside of the van smelled of warm vinyl and the faint salt of years spent beside the ocean. Greg opened the glovebox again and lifted out the folded note.\n\n\"I wrote this a long time ago,\" he said.\n\n\"To who?\"\n\n\"Myself, mostly.\"\n\nHe handed it to me.\n\n\"Would you like me to read it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nThe note was short. It said that if he ever had a home worth coming back to, he should stop pretending he had to leave first. The words were clumsy and heartfelt. It had no signature except his name, written so hard the pen had marked the paper beneath.\n\nI read it twice.\n\n\"When did you write it?\"\n\n\"After one of the times I was pretty sure everything good was about to fall apart.\"\n\nHe looked at the dashboard.\n\n\"I didn't know what to do with it. I stuck it in here and kept finding it every time I tried to clean.\"\n\nI felt a sharp ache in my chest.\n\n\"Why didn't you throw it away?\"\n\n\"Because I kept thinking maybe someday I'd read it and know what it meant.\"\n\nI folded the paper along its old creases.\n\n\"Do you know now?\"\n\n\"Some days.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Other days I still get scared I have to be the funniest guy in the room, the one everybody can count on, or the one nobody asks too much of. Then I see the note and remember I wanted to stop running.\"\n\nI handed it back.\n\n\"I don't want to be the reason you stop traveling.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I want you to choose when to come back.\"\n\nHe laughed softly.\n\n\"That sounds a lot harder.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nThe rain softened. We carried the old map into a small roadside shelter because the van was too cramped for both of us to spread it across our knees. The paper had marks for places Greg wanted to visit when he was younger, many of them crossed out for reasons he'd forgotten.\n\n\"Did you ever go here?\" I asked, pointing to a town on the coast.\n\n\"No. Ran out of gas money before I got there.\"\n\n\"Do you still want to?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Maybe. Not because I owe it to that guy who wrote the list. Because it looks nice.\"\n\nI took my own notebook from my bag, the one where I tracked voluntary Threadway visits to Lanternweald and the dates I'd promised people.\n\n\"Want to make a new map?\"\n\n\"With actual plans?\"\n\n\"Some actual plans. Some places we might visit. No promises we can't keep.\"\n\nHe liked that.\n\nGreg tapped the date with his pencil.\n\n\"I told Nola I'd play forty minutes,\" he said. \"Not the whole night. She's got other performers coming.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"What if she asks me to stay for cleanup?\"\n\n\"Then you decide whether you're available. Not whether you're a bad person if you leave.\"\n\nHe turned the pencil between his fingers.\n\n\"I used to think leaving was the worst thing I could do to someone. Then I got good at leaving before they asked.\"\n\nI looked toward the van, where the old note had lived for years.\n\n\"Maybe there's a difference between abandoning somebody and finishing what you agreed to do.\"\n\nHe considered that.\n\n\"Forty minutes of music,\" he said. \"And I can still help pack my own gear.\"\n\n\"That's a plan.\"\n\nWe added the return route and the last safe Threadway crossing we knew about. I wrote a question mark beside the time because the Lanternweald tide board could change after heavy rain.\n\nGreg looked at the mark.\n\n\"You're okay with not knowing yet?\"\n\n\"I am if we say that it's uncertain. I don't want to pretend we know something just because the blank part makes us uncomfortable.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"You'd get along with the people in the archive.\"\n\n\"Some of them are unbearable.\"\n\n\"And you love them anyway.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe accepted the answer without needing it softened. Then he drew a star beside the concert and let the rest of the map remain unfinished.\n\nWe marked the next scheduled trip to Starwake Pier, where he had agreed to play a short concert. He wrote a reminder to check whether the stage was available and whether anyone else had already booked it.\n\nThen he pointed to a town on the old map.\n\n\"Could we put that one down as a maybe?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\nWe added a little question mark.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"I like the maybe.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nWe sat quietly for a while, watching rain slide down the shelter roof.\n\n\"Can I ask something?\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you ever worry that leaving for another person means I'm going to stop wanting you back?\"\n\nI thought about it.\n\n\"Sometimes I worry you'll interpret my leaving as a judgment about you. But I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. We both chose this with our eyes open. The answer isn't to stop having other relationships. It's to keep asking what we actually want.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"And what do you want today?\"\n\n\"To finish this map. Then get dinner with you. Then maybe go home and forget the map for a while.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"Very romantic.\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nHe moved closer.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe kissed under the shelter roof while the rain dwindled to a mist. He tasted like coffee, and I felt him smile when I wrapped my arms around him.\n\nBefore leaving the shelter, Greg opened the map again and drew three symbols beside Starwake Pier.\n\n“Lightning bolt?” I asked.\n\n“Power for the stage.”\n\n“Rectangle?”\n\n“Room for the equipment.”\n\n“That... potato?”\n\n“Bathroom. You'd be amazed how fast a beautiful concert becomes a disaster when nobody plans for a bathroom.”\n\n“You made a touring checklist.”\n\n“An extremely glamorous touring checklist. Also says spare cable and money for gas.”\n\nHe hesitated beside the date of his planned concert. “I said yes before asking whether they'd already booked another singer.”\n\n“Then call.”\n\nGreg found the volunteer's number and asked about the schedule. Someone else had booked the afternoon. He didn't demand priority because he was known in another world; he offered an evening set or help with sound if there was room.\n\nWhen he hung up, his smile was crooked with relief. “Look at that. Being the guy with a guitar doesn't mean I have to be the center of the show.”\n\nHe tried to turn the sentiment into a lyric, forgot the rhyme halfway through, and laughed at himself.\n\nThen the van's fuel light caught his eye.\n\n“Speaking of glamorous,” he said, “we should buy gas before I accidentally make this a walking tour.”\n\nI laughed so hard I had to lean against him. He kissed the top of my head, quick and affectionate, and let the old letter remain exactly what it was: a piece of his past, not a script for every choice we made next.\n\n\nWe drove home with the new map folded carefully beside the old note. We didn't cover the past. We made room for it to travel without choosing our destination.\n\nAt home Greg placed the note in his song notebook rather than hiding it in the glovebox again.\n\nBefore he closed the notebook, he showed me the back of the old paper.\n\nThere was a tiny penciled list: a place to sleep, enough money for fuel, one person he could call without rehearsing an apology. He'd crossed out the third line and written it again.\n\n\"I didn't remember that part,\" he said.\n\n\"What do you think it meant?\"\n\n\"That I used to think needing somebody made me a burden.\"\n\nI rested my hand beside his, not on the paper.\n\n\"Do you still think it?\"\n\n\"Some days. Less often when I ask and get a real answer.\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"Can I ask you for next weekend? Not forever. Just a day.\"\n\n\"Yes. Let's check the calendar before we decide which day.\"\n\nWe did. The Sunday afternoon was free. He wrote it beside the Starwake performance, with enough time between the entries that a delayed crossing wouldn't ruin both plans.\n\nIt wasn't a perfect promise.\n\nIt was one we understood.\n\nGreg closed the notebook and put it on the shelf where he kept the music he was still working on. The old note had stopped being a secret instruction and become part of a story he was willing to read again.\n\nThe map went on the wall.\n\nThe first place marked on it was home.\n\nThe second was a question mark.","word_count":1503},"su-46":{"number":46,"title":"The Listening Fair","content":"Toph did not like the name of the Listening Fair.\n\n\"Sounds like a competition for people with ears,\" she said.\n\nGreg laughed.\n\n\"Well, technically—\"\n\n\"Finish that sentence and I'll make you eat your guitar.\"\n\nI tried very hard to look professional.\n\nThe fair was a small event at Starwake Pier, arranged to raise money for repairing the western boardwalk and teaching people how to recognize the sounds of an unsafe structure. It wasn't a concert, though Greg had offered music. Toph had agreed to demonstrate how she sensed movement through the earth and stone. Iroh was bringing tea and a few simple percussion instruments for visitors who wanted to join in.\n\nThe trouble was that everyone had a different idea of what listening meant.\n\nGreg wanted people to hear how a melody changed when it made room for another sound. Toph wanted them to pay attention to the floor beneath their feet. Iroh wanted people to notice what was happening before they decided how to respond.\n\nI wanted them to stop arguing over the program.\n\n\"Why don't we ask the people coming?\" I said.\n\nToph folded her arms.\n\n\"Because they haven't come yet.\"\n\n\"Then maybe we should stop pretending we know what they'll need.\"\n\nGreg grinned.\n\n\"That's a pretty good lyric.\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nWe set up three stations along the safe section of the pier. The first had a low table of wooden objects that made different sounds when tapped. The second had a practice platform where Toph could demonstrate vibration without anyone walking onto the damaged western boards. The third was a small listening circle, with chairs arranged so people could choose whether to participate or simply sit.\n\nIroh placed a kettle near the entrance.\n\n\"That is a great deal of noise for a listening fair,\" he observed.\n\n\"Thank you,\" Toph said. \"Finally, someone sensible.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Perhaps the noise will teach us what quiet can offer.\"\n\nToph groaned.\n\n\"Never mind.\"\n\nBy noon the fair had begun.\n\nA child from the square discovered that a hollow wooden block made a deep note while a smaller solid one clicked sharply. An older visitor tapped a cup and asked why the sound changed when it was filled with water. Iroh explained the vibration of the vessel without turning the answer into a lecture about life.\n\nGreg watched the small group with interest.\n\n\"Can I try something?\" he asked.\n\nToph tilted her head.\n\n\"Depends.\"\n\nHe played three quiet notes on his guitar, then stopped.\n\nThe sound faded into the air.\n\n\"Now,\" he said, \"what did you hear after the last note?\"\n\nAt first nobody answered.\n\nThen Edda pointed toward the far railing.\n\n\"The wind through the gaps.\"\n\nPip said they heard the water under the boards.\n\nA child said they heard someone laughing by the tea stand.\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nToph looked toward him.\n\n\"That was actually good.\"\n\nHe blinked.\n\n\"Thanks?\"\n\n\"Don't get used to it.\"\n\nI watched them grin at each other and felt the strange pleasure of seeing people I cared about find a way to work together without having to become the same person.\n\nAt the practice platform, Toph explained how she could sense small changes through the stone beneath her feet. She did not claim to hear every sound or know every danger.\n\n\"Earth tells me some things,\" she said. \"Wood tells me less. And if you need to know whether a railing is safe, you still check it with proper tools.\"\n\nDanse, who had come to supervise the temporary supports, nodded.\n\n\"Correct.\"\n\nToph smirked.\n\n\"Write that down, tin man.\"\n\nHe looked as though he wanted to object.\n\nThen he smiled.\n\nIroh watched the exchange over his cup.\n\n\"Learning to listen can make even a disagreement useful.\"\n\n\"Don't put that on a sign,\" Toph said.\n\n\"Too late,\" Greg whispered to me.\n\nHe had already written it in his notebook.\n\nA boy at the fair asked whether the low note he'd heard beneath the stage meant the old underground passage was finally opening.\n\n\"No,\" Tamsin said. \"What we heard is a sound we can record. We don't yet know its cause well enough to call it a safe passage.\"\n\nThe boy looked disappointed.\n\nGreg crouched beside the sound board. \"Sometimes a guitar makes a note because the string's loose. Sometimes it's the room. You don't just decide the wall is singing because that sounds cooler.\"\n\nToph snorted. \"Now that is advice.\"\n\nShe invited the boy to place his palm on the marked safe stone where the crew had prepared a vibration demonstration. He noticed the difference between the controlled tapping and the ordinary movement from people walking nearby. He didn't need to break a wall or enter a restricted tunnel to understand that small changes could matter.\n\nIroh kept the tea cups away from the equipment. Fen showed a public tide chart, with the closed sections clearly marked. Nola checked that the quiet listening area was still accessible after somebody moved a chair to watch the demonstration.\n\nI watched the fair become something more interesting than a competition over who was the wisest person in the room. Greg could describe the music, Toph could describe the stone, and the engineers could say what they'd tested. None of those descriptions was a substitute for the others.\n\nWhen a volunteer asked whether we could leave the demonstration running overnight, Nola said no. The equipment would be stored during the scheduled shift, and the western pier would remain closed until the inspection was complete.\n\nGreg put his guitar away and came to stand beside me.\n\n\"Was that a good fair?\" he asked.\n\n\"I think people learned to ask better questions.\"\n\n\"And nobody made me play the grief song.\"\n\n\"That's a very good outcome too.\"\n\nHe smiled, touched my fingers, and waited. I took his hand. We had other places to go and people to love, but for one quiet moment the sound of the water and his warmth beside me were enough.\n\nThe afternoon was not perfect. One guest demanded that Greg play the old grief-song he'd refused at the Lantern Walk. Greg said no and offered another piece. The guest left disappointed. Nobody chased them.\n\nAnother visitor wanted Toph to demonstrate her abilities by breaking a stone wall. She declined because the point of the fair was noticing damage before someone got hurt, not creating new damage for entertainment.\n\nIroh had to close the tea station early when the last kettle emptied.\n\nNo one could conjure more water from politeness.\n\nHalfway through the fair, Greg's speaker made a noise like an angry swarm of mechanical bees.\n\n“New genre,” he announced, reaching for the mixer. “Terrible debut.”\n\nHe muted the channel before touching the connections. The technician in the yellow jacket checked the lead and found fraying near one end.\n\n“My lucky cable,” Greg said mournfully.\n\n“Your lucky cable is retired,” she replied, attaching a DO NOT USE tag.\n\nToph planted a heel on the safe platform. “I felt that thing rattling through the boards. Are we finally done with the terrible noise?”\n\n“I was considering a memorial concert.”\n\n“You were not.”\n\nIroh passed by with a tray of cups. “Perhaps a moment of respectful silence would be more appropriate.”\n\nGreg laughed. “See? This guy gets it.”\n\nBut Iroh didn't try to repair the speaker or deliver an explanation. The technician replaced the cable, Greg thanked her by name, and Toph used the vibrations she'd felt to explain why sound could reveal a problem without identifying its exact source.\n\nWhen the music returned, Greg played a deliberately silly three-note flourish for the technician. She rolled her eyes and took a bow.\n\nIt struck me how different their ways of caring were. Toph found the thing nobody wanted to admit was wrong. Greg used humor to make a mistake survivable, then got his hands dirty fixing it. Iroh watched the room and made sure the people doing the work had what they needed. Nobody gave a speech about the meaning of life.\n\n\nThe fair ended with a small group sitting by the repaired railings. Greg played a slow melody. Iroh tapped a gentle rhythm on the rim of a wooden cup. Toph placed one hand on the safe stone platform and followed the beat through her fingers.\n\nFor a while they matched each other.\n\nThen Greg stopped playing.\n\nThe rhythm continued, soft and uneven, carried by Iroh's cup and the sound of people shifting in their chairs. The water beneath the pier made its own quiet music.\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"That's what I was trying to write.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"The part where you stop?\"\n\n\"The part where stopping doesn't mean the song's over.\"\n\nI felt a small ache of affection.\n\nAfterward we counted the donations. The fair had raised enough for new fasteners and part of the boardwalk lumber, but not the entire repair. We wrote the amount clearly on the notice board. Tamsin would order only what the fund could cover, and the damaged area would stay closed until the remaining money was found.\n\nGreg set down his guitar.\n\n\"I think that was worth doing.\"\n\n\"It was,\" I said.\n\nHe looked at the empty chairs.\n\n\"Even though nobody got a big show?\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nIroh packed the cups, Toph collected her practice stones, and the volunteers signed off on the cleanup rota. Nobody disappeared before putting away the equipment they'd agreed to carry.\n\nWhen the pier was finally quiet, Greg and I stood by the water.\n\n\"How was I?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very talented.\"\n\n\"Only talented?\"\n\n\"Annoyingly talented.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"That's better.\"\n\nThen he grew serious.\n\n\"Thanks for not pushing me to play something I wasn't ready to play.\"\n\n\"You get to decide what your songs mean.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI took his hand.\n\nThe evening air was cool, and the last light had turned the water silver. He leaned close enough that I could smell the faint scent of guitar strings and salt on his clothes.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis kiss was warm, gentle, and a little shy. We stayed by the railing until the first lanterns came on, listening to the sounds the fair had taught us not to overlook.\n\nThe pier still needed work.\n\nSo did we, sometimes.\n\nBut I liked the quiet between the notes.","word_count":1713}}};for(const story of library.series){const entries=changes[story.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of story.chapters){const e=entries[ch.id];if(!e||e.number!==ch.chapter_number)continue;ch.content=e.content;ch.title=e.title;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}story.total_word_count=story.chapters.reduce((sum,ch)=>sum+(Number(ch.word_count)||0),0);}})();
